 To find the volume of the composite solid, we need to break it down into simpler shapes whose volumes we can calculate and then sum them up.

1. **Identify the shapes:**
   - The solid consists of a rectangular prism and a smaller rectangular prism on top of it.

2. **Determine the dimensions:**
   - The base of the larger rectangular prism is \(16 \text{ cm} \times 7 \text{ cm}\).
   - The height of the larger rectangular prism is \(7 \text{ cm}\).
   - The smaller rectangular prism on top has the same height as the base of the larger prism, which is \(7 \text{ cm}\), and the same width, which is \(7 \text{ cm}\). The length of the smaller prism is the same as the height of the larger prism, which is \(7 \text{ cm}\).

3. **Calculate the volume of the larger rectangular prism:**
   \[
   \text{Volume of the larger prism} = \text{length} \times \text{width} \times \text{height} = 16 \text{ cm} \times 7 \text{ cm} \times 7 \text{ cm} = 784 \text{ cm}^3
   \]

4. **Calculate the volume of the smaller rectangular prism:**
   \[
   \text{Volume of the smaller prism} = \text{length} \times \text{width} \times \text{height} = 7 \text{ cm} \times 7 \text{ cm} \times 7 \text{ cm} = 343 \text{ cm}^3
   \]

5. **Sum the volumes of the two prisms:**
   \[
   \text{Total volume} = \text{Volume of the larger prism} + \text{Volume of the smaller prism} = 784 \text{ cm}^3 + 343 \text{ cm}^3 = 1127 \text{ cm}^3
   \]

